{"text": "MANE NOBISCUM DOMINE }\n\nIn Memoriam: Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr. (1968 \u2014 2018)\n\nFirty YEARS LATER\n\nOn the Wednesday after Easter, April 4, 2018 we will mark the 50\u2122 anniversary of the\ncruel murder of Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr. After the passage of so many years, we realize\nthat for many American citizens this death is only a matter of distant history. For them, the\nonly tangible consequence of this act of senseless violence is the fact that they have a day off\nevery January 15\". However, for those who are attentive to history with an awareness of the\nracial divide in the United States, this sad anniversary is a somber reminder of a \u201cdream\ndeferred\u201d.\n\nI encourage every parish to find appropriate ways to reflect upon Dr. King\u2019s life,\nprophetic teachings, and untimely death. There are many ways of doing this: appropriate\npetitions in the Prayers of the Faithful on Palm Sunday, on Holy Thursday, on Good Friday,\non Easter Sunday, or the Octave of Easter; a brief bulletin article making use of the\nattached material or any one of the many resources available online. It is also possible to\nfind a place to make appropriate references to the need for Catholics to renew their\ncommitment to the ongoing struggle for peace, justice, and racial harmony in homilies\ndelivered on Palm Sunday, or Holy Thursday, or Good Friday, or Easter Sunday, or the\nOctave of Easter. All these days call every Christian to contemplate the Resurrection of the\nPrince of Peace and to renew his or her commitment to live by the Gospel of Jesus Christ.",
